\chapter{\texorpdfstring{\texttt{BinExpr}, \texttt{UnaryExpr}, and Fused
Evaluation}{, , and Fused Evaluation}}\label{ch16-binexpr-unaryexpr-and-fused-evaluation}

Chapter 15 introduced the expression-template facility of MuDSL as a
mechanism for building lazy arithmetic trees over types that derive from
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL} with the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ExprTemplates} feature. The
chapter ended at the point where overloaded operators return opaque node
types, deferred until the caller explicitly asks for a result. This
chapter opens those node types and explains exactly how evaluation
proceeds.

Two node types form the spine of every expression tree:
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}Op,\allowbreak{} L,\allowbreak{} R\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
returned by every binary operator, and
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}Op,\allowbreak{} E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
returned by unary negation. Both are trivial aggregates that store their
operands and an operation tag, and both expose a \texttt{constexpr}
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member that walks the subtree
in a single fused pass. Understanding these two types is sufficient to
predict the behaviour of any expression built through the
\texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin.

The chapter also covers the operation tag types supplied by the standard
library, the \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} helper that unifies
leaf and inner-node evaluation, the \texttt{ExprLike} concept that
constrains the leaves, and the free-function operator overloads that
permit \texttt{BinExpr} nodes to appear on the left-hand side of further
operators. Worked examples range from scalar arithmetic to element-wise
vector operations, and a closing section catalogues the pitfalls that
accompany reference-deferred evaluation.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Recap: The \texttt{ExprTemplates}
Feature}{Recap: The  Feature}}\label{ch16-recap-the-exprtemplates-feature}

Before describing the node types it is useful to recap the surface API
presented by the \texttt{ExprTemplates} feature, introduced in Chapter
15. A DSL type derives from
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL\textless{}\allowbreak{}Derived,\allowbreak{} dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ExprTemplates\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and supplies a
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member
that returns the underlying value. The feature's mixin injects binary
\texttt{operator+}, \texttt{operator-}, \texttt{operator*}, and
\texttt{operator/\allowbreak{}} as hidden friends, plus a unary
\texttt{operator-} for negation. None of these operators compute a
result; each constructs and returns a node.

The node types live at namespace scope in \texttt{dsl} so that they can
be mentioned in user code when needed, although in practice
\texttt{auto} is almost always sufficient. The binary operators return
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Derived,\allowbreak{} R\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and analogous specialisations, while unary negation returns
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Derived\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The operators are \texttt{constexpr}, so the resulting tree can be built
at compile time.

\begin{code}
struct Scalar : dsl::DSL<Scalar, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  double v{};
  Scalar() = default;
  explicit Scalar(double x) : v(x) {}
  double expr_value() const { return v; }
};

Scalar a{2}, b{3}, c{4};
auto expr = a + b * c - a;   // type: BinExpr<std::minus<>, BinExpr<std::plus<>, Scalar, BinExpr<std::multiplies<>, Scalar, Scalar>>, Scalar>
double r = expr.eval();      // 2 + 3*4 - 2 = 12
\end{code}

The example above, drawn from
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}12-expression-te\allowbreak{}mplates.\allowbreak{}cpp},
exhibits the central contract of this chapter: the operators build a
tree, and \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} collapses it into a
single value. The remainder of the chapter explains how that collapse
happens.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{BinExpr} Node
Type}{The  Node Type}}\label{ch16-the-binexpr-node-type}

\texttt{BinExpr} is the node type returned by every binary operator in
the \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin. It is a class template with three
parameters: the operation tag \texttt{Op}, the left operand type
\texttt{L}, and the right operand type \texttt{R}. The class stores
copies of both operands as members named \texttt{lhs} and \texttt{rhs},
and constructs them by move from the constructor's by-value parameters.

\begin{code}
template <typename Op, typename L, typename R> struct BinExpr
{
  L lhs;
  R rhs;

  constexpr BinExpr (L l, R r) : lhs (std::move (l)), rhs (std::move (r)) {}
  // ... eval() described below ...
};
\end{code}

Because \texttt{L} and \texttt{R} are deduced from the arguments passed
to the operator, a \texttt{BinExpr} node can hold leaves of the derived
DSL type, other \texttt{BinExpr} nodes, or \texttt{UnaryExpr} nodes, all
uniformly. The node itself is an aggregate with no further logic beyond
construction and evaluation, which keeps its layout minimal and makes
deeply nested trees cheap to instantiate.

The operation tag \texttt{Op} is not stored as a data member; it is a
type parameter only. The node carries the operation in its static type,
and at evaluation time a temporary \texttt{Op\{\}} is constructed and
invoked. This is the standard tag/functor pattern:
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}divides\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
are all empty classes whose call operator performs the corresponding
arithmetic. Carrying the operation in the type system rather than in a
data member keeps every \texttt{BinExpr} the size of just its two
operands.

The constructor is \texttt{constexpr}, so a \texttt{BinExpr} can be
assembled during constant evaluation. The members \texttt{lhs} and
\texttt{rhs} are public, which permits introspection of an expression
tree by generic code --- for example, a visitor that walks the tree for
diagnostic purposes can read \texttt{node.\allowbreak{}lhs} and
\texttt{node.\allowbreak{}rhs} directly. The primary intended access
path, however, is \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{UnaryExpr} Node
Type}{The  Node Type}}\label{ch16-the-unaryexpr-node-type}

\texttt{UnaryExpr} is the companion node type for unary operations. The
\texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin provides only one unary operator, negation,
which returns
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Derived\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Like \texttt{BinExpr}, the class template is parametrised by an
operation tag and a single operand type \texttt{E}, and stores the
operand by value in a public member named \texttt{operand}.

\begin{code}
template <typename Op, typename E> struct UnaryExpr
{
  E operand;

  explicit constexpr UnaryExpr (E e) : operand (std::move (e)) {}
  // ... eval() described below ...
};
\end{code}

The constructor is marked \texttt{explicit} to prevent implicit
conversions from an operand into a unary expression, reflecting the fact
that constructing a node is a deliberate act performed by an operator
rather than by ordinary conversion. As with \texttt{BinExpr}, the
operation tag is carried in the static type and is never stored as a
data member.

Although the library only ships
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
as a unary tag, the \texttt{UnaryExpr} template is general and will
accept any callable taking a single argument. A user who wishes to
introduce, for example, a logical-not node for a boolean DSL can
construct
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}my\_\allowbreak{}not\_\allowbreak{}t,\allowbreak{} Bool\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\{x\}}
directly, or supply a custom operator that returns such a node. The
evaluation machinery described below is agnostic to the choice of tag.

\texttt{UnaryExpr} participates in the same recursive evaluation as
\texttt{BinExpr}. When a \texttt{UnaryExpr} appears as the operand of a
\texttt{BinExpr}, the binary node's \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand}
helper detects the inner node's
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method and recurses into it.
No special handling is required to mix unary and binary nodes in a
single tree.

\section{Operation Tags}\label{ch16-operation-tags}

The operation tags used by the \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin are drawn
from the standard library.
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
represents addition,
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
subtraction,
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
multiplication,
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}divides\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
division, and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
unary negation. Each is an empty class with a \texttt{constexpr} call
operator that forwards to the corresponding operator expression, and the
transparent specialisation
(\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
rather than
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}void\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}})
deduces its argument and return types from the call site.

The choice of standard-library tags has two consequences. First, the
operation applied at evaluation is exactly
\texttt{Op\{\}(\allowbreak{}l,\allowbreak{} r)\allowbreak{}} for binary
nodes and \texttt{Op\{\}(\allowbreak{}x)\allowbreak{}} for unary nodes,
which means that any type for which \texttt{operator+} and friends are
defined is usable as a leaf. Second, because the tags are empty, they
impose no size overhead on the node, and because they are trivial they
participate cleanly in \texttt{constexpr} evaluation.

A tag must be defined for the value type returned by the leaves. For
\texttt{Scalar} leaves, the value type is \texttt{double}, and all five
standard tags are defined for \texttt{double}. For a vector leaf whose
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}float\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
the same tags work provided
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}float\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
supports the corresponding operator --- and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector} does not provide
element-wise arithmetic out of the box, so a user-defined vector type
with overloaded operators is required. The chapter returns to this point
in the worked examples.

The tags are invoked by value-construction: \texttt{Op\{\}} constructs a
temporary and immediately calls its call operator. This is the same
pattern used by \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}transform} and
the standard algorithms, so the semantics will be familiar. The tags are
not stored, not configured, and not user-visible at the leaf level; they
are purely an internal dispatch mechanism.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
Method: Verbatim
Definition}{The  Method: Verbatim Definition}}\label{ch16-the-eval-method-verbatim-definition}

The heart of this chapter is the
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member of \texttt{BinExpr}.
Its definition is short, and is reproduced verbatim from
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp}:

\begin{code}
  constexpr auto
  eval () const
  {
    auto l = eval_operand (lhs);
    auto r = eval_operand (rhs);
    return Op{}(l, r);
  }
\end{code}

The method is \texttt{constexpr} and \texttt{const}-qualified, so it may
be invoked on temporaries and during constant evaluation. It evaluates
the left operand, then the right operand, then applies the operation tag
to the pair. The order of evaluation of the two operands is the order in
which they appear in the source: left, then right. This is a stronger
guarantee than that provided by the built-in arithmetic operators, whose
operand evaluation order is unspecified for most operators.

The corresponding member of \texttt{UnaryExpr} is structurally
identical, with a single operand:

\begin{code}
  constexpr auto
  eval () const
  {
    if constexpr (requires { operand.eval (); })
      {
        return Op{}(operand.eval ());
      }
    else if constexpr (requires { operand.expr_value (); })
      {
        return Op{}(operand.expr_value ());
      }
    else
      {
        return Op{}(operand);
      }
  }
\end{code}

The \texttt{UnaryExpr} version inlines the dispatch that
\texttt{BinExpr} factors into the \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand}
helper. Both formulations are equivalent in effect: evaluate the
operand, then apply \texttt{Op} to the result. The duplication is
deliberate, keeping each node type self-contained.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand}
Helper}{The  Helper}}\label{ch16-the-eval_operand-helper}

\texttt{BinExpr} factors operand evaluation into a private static
helper, \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand}, templated on the operand
type. The helper uses \texttt{if constexpr} with \texttt{requires}
clauses to dispatch on three cases, in order: if the operand exposes an
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method, call it; otherwise, if
it exposes an
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method,
call that; otherwise, return the operand unchanged.

\begin{code}
  template <typename T>
  static constexpr auto
  eval_operand (const T &operand)
  {
    if constexpr (requires { operand.eval (); })
      {
        return operand.eval ();
      }
    else if constexpr (requires { operand.expr_value (); })
      {
        return operand.expr_value ();
      }
    else
      {
        return operand;
      }
  }
\end{code}

The first branch handles inner nodes --- both \texttt{BinExpr} and
\texttt{UnaryExpr} expose \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, so
a subtree is collapsed by a recursive call. The second branch handles
leaves of the derived DSL type, which expose
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} but not
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The third branch handles raw
values that have been woven into the tree, such as a plain
\texttt{double} literal used as an operand; such a value is returned
unchanged and is then passed directly to the operation tag.

The use of \texttt{requires} expressions makes the dispatch structural
rather than tag-based. Any type that exposes an
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method will be treated as an
inner node, regardless of whether it is one of MuDSL's node types. This
means a user-defined node type that supplies a conforming
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} can participate in the same
tree without modification to the library. The same openness applies to
leaves: any type with an
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method can
serve as a leaf, even if it does not derive from
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL}.

The third branch is what permits raw values to appear in expressions.
Although the \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin only defines operators with
the derived type on the left, the free-function overloads described
later in the chapter accept any right-hand operand, including built-in
numeric types. When such a raw value reaches
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand}, the third branch returns it
unchanged, and the operation tag is then applied to the pair of
evaluated values.

\section{Recursive Evaluation: A Single Fused
Pass}\label{ch16-recursive-evaluation-a-single-fused-pass}

Evaluation proceeds by structural recursion from the root. The root
node's \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} calls
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} on each of its children; for each
child that is itself a node, \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} calls
the child's \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, which recurses in
turn. The recursion terminates at leaves, where
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} takes the
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} branch and
returns the underlying value. The operation tags are applied on the way
back up the recursion, so the root's \texttt{Op} is applied last.

Consider the expression \texttt{a + b * c}. The multiplication binds
tighter than the addition, so the tree is
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Calling \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} on the root evaluates
the left leaf \texttt{a} through
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, then
evaluates the right subtree by calling its
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, which in turn evaluates
\texttt{b} and \texttt{c} and applies
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Finally the root applies
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to \texttt{a} and the product \texttt{b * c}. The total work is one
multiplication and one addition, in that order.

Because each node applies its operation after its children have been
fully evaluated, the entire tree is walked in a single post-order pass.
There is no staging: no intermediate vector of partial results is
assembled, and no sub-expression is evaluated more than once. The pass
is what the literature calls \emph{fused}: the operations are combined
at the point of evaluation rather than materialised as separate results.

The recursion depth is proportional to the height of the tree, which for
a left-associative chain of \texttt{n} binary operators is \texttt{n}.
For typical MuDSL usage, where expressions are short, this depth is
negligible. For very long chains, the recursion is still bounded by the
expression's syntactic nesting, not by any data structure size, so stack
exhaustion is not a practical concern.

\section{Fusion Versus Eager
Evaluation}\label{ch16-fusion-versus-eager-evaluation}

The defining contrast between expression templates and eager evaluation
is the treatment of intermediate results. In eager evaluation, each
operator computes and materialises a result: \texttt{a + b * c} would
first compute \texttt{b * c} into a temporary, then compute
\texttt{a + temp} into a second temporary. For scalar types the
temporaries are cheap, but for vector or matrix types each temporary is
a heap allocation and a full-length copy.

With \texttt{BinExpr} and \texttt{UnaryExpr}, no temporary is
materialised for any sub-expression. The fused
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} pass reads each leaf once and
combines the values on the stack. For a vector expression of length
\texttt{n} with \texttt{k} operators, eager evaluation would allocate
\texttt{k} temporaries of size \texttt{n}; the fused pass allocates
none. The result is a single value, constructed in place from the
leaves.

\begin{code}
struct Vec3 : dsl::DSL<Vec3, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  std::array<float,3> v{};
  Vec3() = default;
  Vec3(float x, float y, float z) : v{x, y, z} {}
  std::array<float,3> expr_value() const { return v; }
};

Vec3 u{1,2,3}, w{4,5,6};
auto sum = u + w;                       // BinExpr<std::plus<>, Vec3, Vec3>
auto r = sum.eval();                    // std::array<float,3>{5,7,9}
\end{code}

This example relies on
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}array\textless{}\allowbreak{}float,\allowbreak{}3\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
supporting \texttt{operator+}, which it does not in the standard
library. A real vector type would either provide its own
\texttt{operator+} returning a new vector, or would use a custom
operation tag. The principle, however, is independent of the value type:
the fused pass applies the tag once per node, with no intermediate
storage.

The fusion guarantee is a property of the
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} implementation, not of the
type system. Because \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} calls
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} on inner nodes immediately and
applies the operation tag before returning, there is no point at which a
sub-expression's result is stored except in the local variables
\texttt{l} and \texttt{r} of the calling node. These locals are
registers or small stack slots for scalar types, and move-constructed
values for larger types.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The Return Type of
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}}{The Return Type of }}\label{ch16-the-return-type-of-eval}

The return type of \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is deduced
by \texttt{auto} from the body of the method. For a \texttt{BinExpr}
whose leaves return \texttt{double} from
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, the
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} calls yield \texttt{double}, the
operation tag
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
returns \texttt{double}, and \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
returns \texttt{double}. For a
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
the same reasoning yields \texttt{double}.

The return type is therefore the common type of the operation tag's
result over the operand value types. For homogeneous trees --- all
leaves of the same type --- this is simply the leaf value type. For
heterogeneous trees, where leaves of different types are combined, the
return type is whatever
\texttt{Op\{\}(\allowbreak{}l,\allowbreak{} r)\allowbreak{}} yields,
which follows the usual arithmetic-conversion rules of the operation
being invoked.

\begin{code}
Scalar a{2}, b{3};
auto e1 = a + b;                       // BinExpr<std::plus<>, Scalar, Scalar>
static_assert(std::is_same_v<decltype(e1.eval()), double>);

auto e2 = -a;                          // UnaryExpr<std::negate<>, Scalar>
static_assert(std::is_same_v<decltype(e2.eval()), double>);
\end{code}

Because the return type is deduced, it is not possible to write a
forward declaration of \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} without
a trailing return type. Users who need to name the return type should
use
\texttt{decltype(\allowbreak{}expr.\allowbreak{}eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
rather than attempting to spell it out. This is the same idiom used
throughout the standard library for deduced-return-type functions.

The return type is preserved across nesting. A \texttt{BinExpr} whose
right operand is a \texttt{BinExpr} returns the type of the outer
operation applied to the leaf type and the inner operation's return
type. As long as the operations are defined for the relevant types, the
deduction chains cleanly and the final return type is the leaf value
type (or its arithmetic-promoted equivalent).

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{ExprLike}
Concept}{The  Concept}}\label{ch16-the-exprlike-concept}

MuDSL provides a concept, \texttt{ExprLike}, that characterises types
usable as leaves in an expression template tree. The concept is defined
near the bottom of \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} and requires two
things: the type must have the \texttt{ExprTemplates} feature, and it
must expose a \texttt{const}-qualified
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
concept ExprLike = HasFeature<T, ExprTemplates>
                   && requires (const T &t) { t.expr_value (); };
\end{code}

The first clause,
\texttt{HasFeature\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{} ExprTemplates\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
is the feature-tag check introduced in Chapter 5: it verifies that
\texttt{T} derives from
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{} dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ExprTemplates\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(or otherwise registers the feature). The second clause is a
\texttt{requires} expression that checks the existence of
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. Together
they capture exactly the contract that the \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin
imposes on its derived class.

\texttt{ExprLike} is the bridge between the user-facing DSL type and the
internal node machinery. Although \texttt{BinExpr} and
\texttt{UnaryExpr} do not themselves constrain their operands with
\texttt{ExprLike} --- they accept any type that supports the required
methods --- the concept is the intended way to document and constrain
generic code that builds expressions. A function that accepts a leaf and
returns an expression can constrain its parameter with \texttt{ExprLike}
to give clear diagnostics when passed an inappropriate type.

The concept does not constrain the return type of
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. Any
return type is acceptable, on the understanding that the operation tags
used in the tree must be defined for that type. This keeps the concept
structural and avoids coupling it to a particular value type. A DSL
whose leaves return \texttt{int} and a DSL whose leaves return a
user-defined matrix type both satisfy \texttt{ExprLike} equally.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{ExprTerminal}
Tag}{The  Tag}}\label{ch16-the-exprterminal-tag}

\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} declares a small empty struct,
\texttt{ExprTerminal}, that serves as a tag type indicating a terminal
in an expression template tree. The tag itself is not used by the
\texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin or by the node types described in this
chapter; it is provided as a hook for user code that wishes to mark or
detect leaves by trait.

\begin{code}
struct ExprTerminal
{
};
\end{code}

A user-defined leaf type may, if it wishes, derive from
\texttt{ExprTerminal} or specialise a trait on it to integrate with
custom tree-walking code. The library's own evaluation machinery does
not consult the tag: the \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} helper
dispatches on the presence of \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
and \texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
methods, not on inheritance from \texttt{ExprTerminal}. The tag is
therefore a documentation and extension point rather than a functional
requirement.

The existence of \texttt{ExprTerminal} in the header is a hint that the
library anticipates third-party extension. A tree visitor that wishes to
distinguish leaves from inner nodes generically can check
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}is\_\allowbreak{}base\_\allowbreak{}of\_\allowbreak{}v\textless{}\allowbreak{}ExprTerminal,\allowbreak{} T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
provided the leaf types cooperate by deriving from the tag. Leaves
written in the canonical style of \texttt{Scalar} do not need to do so,
because the \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} dispatch already
distinguishes them by method signature.

In practice, most user code will never mention \texttt{ExprTerminal}. It
is mentioned here for completeness, and so that readers of the header
are not surprised by its presence. The functional contract of a leaf is
the \texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method
plus the \texttt{ExprTemplates} feature, neither of which involves the
terminal tag.

\section{\texorpdfstring{\texttt{BinExpr} on the Left: Free-Function
Overloads}{ on the Left: Free-Function Overloads}}\label{ch16-binexpr-on-the-left-free-function-overloads}

The \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin defines its binary operators as hidden
friends inside the derived class. This means the operators fire when the
derived type appears on the left of the operator, but they do not fire
when a \texttt{BinExpr} node appears on the left. Without further
support, an expression like
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}a + b)\allowbreak{} * c} would fail to compile:
the left operand is a \texttt{BinExpr}, not a \texttt{Scalar}, and no
\texttt{operator*} accepts a \texttt{BinExpr} on the left.

MuDSL closes this gap with four free-function operator overloads at
namespace scope, one for each binary operator. Each overload accepts a
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}Op1,\allowbreak{} L1,\allowbreak{} R1\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
on the left and an arbitrary right operand, and returns a new
\texttt{BinExpr} whose left operand is the inner \texttt{BinExpr}.

\begin{code}
template <typename Op1, typename L1, typename R1, typename R>
constexpr auto
operator* (const BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1> &lhs, const R &rhs)
{
  return BinExpr<std::multiplies<>, BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1>, R>{ lhs, rhs };
}
\end{code}

The four overloads cover \texttt{operator+}, \texttt{operator-},
\texttt{operator*}, and \texttt{operator/\allowbreak{}}. They are
symmetrical in shape: each constructs a \texttt{BinExpr} whose operation
tag is the matching standard-library tag, whose left type is the
incoming \texttt{BinExpr}, and whose right type is the deduced
\texttt{R}. The inner \texttt{BinExpr} is copied into the new node by
value.

These overloads are what make left-associative chains like
\texttt{a + b * c - a} evaluate correctly. The expression parses as
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}a + (\allowbreak{}b * c)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} - a}:
the inner \texttt{b * c} is a \texttt{BinExpr}, which then appears on
the left of the subtraction. The free-function \texttt{operator-} for
\texttt{BinExpr} on the left fires and wraps the inner node in an outer
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} .\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Without the free functions, such chains would have to be written with
explicit nesting.

The free functions only cover \texttt{BinExpr} on the left; they do not
cover \texttt{UnaryExpr} on the left. An expression like
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}-a)\allowbreak{} + b} parses as
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} + Scalar},
and the \texttt{operator+} hidden friend of \texttt{Scalar} does not
fire because the left operand is not a \texttt{Scalar}. In practice this
is rarely a problem because unary negation usually binds to a single
leaf, but it is a limitation worth noting. A user who needs
\texttt{UnaryExpr} on the left can write a custom overload, or can
restructure the expression so that the unary node appears on the right.

\section{Worked Example: Scalar
Arithmetic}\label{ch16-worked-example-scalar-arithmetic}

The simplest non-trivial expression is the one from
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}12-expression-te\allowbreak{}mplates.\allowbreak{}cpp}.
Three \texttt{Scalar} leaves are combined with three operators, and the
result is printed.

\begin{code}
struct Scalar : dsl::DSL<Scalar, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  double v{};
  Scalar() = default;
  explicit Scalar(double x) : v(x) {}
  double expr_value() const { return v; }
};

int main() {
  Scalar a{2}, b{3}, c{4};
  auto expr = a + b * c - a;
  std::cout << expr.eval() << "\n";   // prints 12
}
\end{code}

The type of \texttt{expr} is, fully expanded,
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The multiplication is the innermost node, wrapped by the addition,
wrapped by the subtraction. The evaluation walks this tree in a single
pass: \texttt{b * c} is computed first (yielding 12), then
\texttt{a + 12} (yielding 14), then \texttt{14 - a} (yielding 12).

The absence of intermediate temporaries is invisible at the scalar level
because a \texttt{double} temporary is free. The value of the example is
pedagogical: it shows the shape of the tree and the order of operations
in the smallest possible setting. The same shape, applied to a vector
type, is where fusion pays off.

Note that the operands are stored by value inside each \texttt{BinExpr}.
The three \texttt{Scalar} leaves are copied into the inner
multiplication node, the result of which is a node (not a value) that is
copied into the addition node, and so on. The copies are cheap for
\texttt{Scalar} --- eight bytes each --- but for a larger leaf type the
cost of copying the leaf into every node that references it would be
significant. This is the reason the library stores operands by value
rather than by reference: it keeps the nodes self-contained and safe to
return from functions, at the cost of copying leaves that appear
multiple times.

\section{Worked Example: Nested Unary
Negation}\label{ch16-worked-example-nested-unary-negation}

Unary negation inserts a \texttt{UnaryExpr} node into the tree. The
simplest case is negation of a single leaf, but the more interesting
case is negation combined with binary operators, where the recursion
must descend through both node types.

\begin{code}
Scalar a{5}, b{2};
auto expr = -a + b;
std::cout << expr.eval() << "\n";   // prints -3
\end{code}

The type of \texttt{expr} is
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Evaluation of the root calls \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} on the
left, which is a \texttt{UnaryExpr}. The \texttt{UnaryExpr} exposes
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, so
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} calls it; that call in turn
evaluates the inner \texttt{Scalar} leaf through
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and
applies
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to yield \texttt{-5}. The right operand is a plain \texttt{Scalar},
evaluated through
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} to
\texttt{2}. The root applies
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to yield \texttt{-3}.

The example demonstrates that \texttt{UnaryExpr} and \texttt{BinExpr}
interoperate without any special glue. The
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} helper's first branch ---
\texttt{if constexpr (\allowbreak{}requires \{ opera\allowbreak{}nd.\allowbreak{}eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}; \})\allowbreak{}}
--- accepts any type with an \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
method, regardless of whether it is \texttt{BinExpr} or
\texttt{UnaryExpr}. This is the structural dispatch that makes the node
types composable.

A deeper nesting works the same way. An expression like
\texttt{-(\allowbreak{}-a + b)\allowbreak{} * c} produces a tree with a
\texttt{UnaryExpr} at the root, a \texttt{BinExpr} below it, and a
further \texttt{UnaryExpr} inside that. Evaluation still proceeds as a
single post-order walk: the innermost \texttt{UnaryExpr} evaluates
first, then the \texttt{BinExpr}, then the outer \texttt{UnaryExpr},
then the multiplication at the root. Each level applies its operation
tag to the result of its children.

\section{Worked Example: Element-Wise Vector
Arithmetic}\label{ch16-worked-example-element-wise-vector-arithmetic}

To illustrate fusion on a type where intermediates would be costly,
consider a small fixed-size vector type. The leaf's
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns
the vector by value, and the operation tags must be defined for the
vector type. Because \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}array} does
not provide element-wise arithmetic, the example uses a custom
\texttt{Vec} type with overloaded operators.

\begin{code}
struct Vec {
  std::array<float,3> v{};
  Vec() = default;
  Vec(float x, float y, float z) : v{x, y, z} {}
  float& operator[](std::size_t i) { return v[i]; }
  const float& operator[](std::size_t i) const { return v[i]; }
};

constexpr Vec operator+(const Vec& a, const Vec& b) {
  return Vec{a[0]+b[0], a[1]+b[1], a[2]+b[2]};
}
constexpr Vec operator-(const Vec& a, const Vec& b) {
  return Vec{a[0]-b[0], a[1]-b[1], a[2]-b[2]};
}
constexpr Vec operator*(const Vec& a, const Vec& b) {
  return Vec{a[0]*b[0], a[1]*b[1], a[2]*b[2]};
}
constexpr Vec operator-(const Vec& a) {
  return Vec{-a[0], -a[1], -a[2]};
}
\end{code}

With these operators in scope,
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and friends dispatch to the element-wise overloads when invoked on
\texttt{Vec} values. A leaf type wrapping \texttt{Vec} is then
straightforward.

\begin{code}
struct VecLeaf : dsl::DSL<VecLeaf, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  Vec v{};
  VecLeaf() = default;
  VecLeaf(float x, float y, float z) : v{x, y, z} {}
  Vec expr_value() const { return v; }
};

VecLeaf u{1,2,3}, w{4,5,6}, x{7,8,9};
auto expr = u + w * x - u;
Vec r = expr.eval();                  // {1+4*7-1, 2+5*8-2, 3+6*9-3} = {28, 40, 54}
\end{code}

In eager evaluation, the expression \texttt{u + w * x - u} would
allocate three temporary \texttt{Vec} objects: one for \texttt{w * x},
one for \texttt{u + temp1}, and one for \texttt{temp2 - u}. With the
fused \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} pass, no such
temporaries are allocated. The single post-order walk reads each leaf
once and produces the final \texttt{Vec} directly. For a three-element
vector the saving is trivial; for a leaf wrapping a large
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}float\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
it is the difference between \texttt{O(\allowbreak{}k)\allowbreak{}}
allocations and zero.

The example also shows that the operation tags are not coupled to the
leaf type. The \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin hardcodes
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}divides\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
but these transparent functors dispatch to whatever \texttt{operator+}
and so on are found by overload resolution at the call site. A leaf that
returns a custom value type from
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} therefore
gets the right arithmetic for free, provided the corresponding operators
are defined.

\section{\texorpdfstring{\texttt{constexpr}
Evaluation}{ Evaluation}}\label{ch16-constexpr-evaluation}

Every operation in the node types is marked \texttt{constexpr}: the
constructors, the \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} methods, the
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} helper, and the free-function
operator overloads. The operation tags from the standard library are
also \texttt{constexpr}. Consequently, an entire expression tree can be
built and evaluated at compile time, provided the leaf types and their
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} methods
are themselves \texttt{constexpr}.

\begin{code}
constexpr Scalar ca{2}, cb{3}, cc{4};
constexpr auto cexpr = ca + cb * cc - ca;
static_assert(cexpr.eval() == 12);
\end{code}

This capability is what permits expression templates to be used in
template parameters and other constant-expression contexts. A
\texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert} that checks an arithmetic identity,
a non-type template parameter whose value is computed from an
expression, or a \texttt{constexpr} variable initialised from an
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} call are all viable uses.

The requirements for compile-time evaluation are the usual ones: no
non-\texttt{constexpr} operations may appear in the call tree, and all
functions invoked must be reachable at compile time. For the standard
operation tags and built-in arithmetic types these requirements are met
automatically. For a user-defined value type, the type's arithmetic
operators and its
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method
must be \texttt{constexpr}, and the type must be a literal type.

Compile-time evaluation does not change the fused character of the pass.
The recursion unfolds at compile time just as it would at run time, and
the result is a single constant value with no intermediate temporaries
materialised in the compiled program. In favourable cases the compiler
can fold the entire expression into a single constant, eliminating all
trace of the tree from the generated code.

\section{A Larger Expression: Tracing the
Pass}\label{ch16-a-larger-expression-tracing-the-pass}

To consolidate the picture of a single fused pass, consider a slightly
larger expression and trace its evaluation step by step. The expression
is \texttt{a + b * c - a /\allowbreak{} b}, with \texttt{a}, \texttt{b},
\texttt{c} of type \texttt{Scalar}.

\begin{code}
Scalar a{6}, b{3}, c{4};
auto expr = a + b * c - a / b;
std::cout << expr.eval() << "\n";   // 6 + 3*4 - 6/3 = 6 + 12 - 2 = 16
\end{code}

The tree, parsing with standard precedence, is
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}a + (\allowbreak{}b * c)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} - (\allowbreak{}a /\allowbreak{} b)\allowbreak{}}.
The root is a
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
whose left operand is a
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and whose right operand is a
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}divides\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Evaluation proceeds as follows.

The root's \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} calls
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand(\allowbreak{}lhs)\allowbreak{}},
where \texttt{lhs} is the plus-node. The plus-node's
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is invoked, which calls
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} on its own left (the leaf
\texttt{a}, returning 6) and its own right (the multiplication node,
whose \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns 12), and applies
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to yield 18. Control returns to the root, which now calls
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand(\allowbreak{}rhs)\allowbreak{}} on
the divides-node. The divides-node's
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} evaluates \texttt{a} (6) and
\texttt{b} (3) and applies
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}divides\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to yield 2. Finally the root applies
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to 18 and 2, yielding 16.

The trace exhibits three properties that hold for every expression.
First, each leaf is read exactly once per path through the tree; a leaf
that appears twice in the expression (here \texttt{a} and \texttt{b}) is
read once per appearance, not cached. Second, operations are applied in
post-order: children before parents. Third, the entire computation uses
only the local variables \texttt{l} and \texttt{r} of each active
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} frame; no auxiliary storage is
allocated.

The trace also shows that the order of operations is determined entirely
by the structure of the tree, which in turn is determined by the
precedence and associativity of the C++ operators. Because
\texttt{operator*} and \texttt{operator/\allowbreak{}} bind tighter than
\texttt{operator+} and \texttt{operator-}, and because the binary
operators are left-associative, the tree has the shape one would expect
from reading the expression with standard mathematical precedence. There
is no reassociation or optimisation; the tree is evaluated as written.

\section{Mixing Leaves and Raw
Values}\label{ch16-mixing-leaves-and-raw-values}

The third branch of \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} --- the fallback
that returns the operand unchanged --- allows raw values to participate
in expressions. Although the \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin's
hidden-friend operators require a \texttt{Derived} on the left, the
free-function overloads accept any right-hand operand, including a
built-in numeric literal. The result is a \texttt{BinExpr} whose right
operand type is a raw scalar.

\begin{code}
Scalar a{2};
auto expr = a * 3.0;                  // BinExpr<std::multiplies<>, Scalar, double>
std::cout << expr.eval() << "\n";     // 6.0
\end{code}

When \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} runs,
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand(\allowbreak{}lhs)\allowbreak{}} calls
\texttt{a.\allowbreak{}expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
and yields \texttt{2.\allowbreak{}0};
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand(\allowbreak{}rhs)\allowbreak{}} takes
the third branch and returns \texttt{3.\allowbreak{}0} unchanged.
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\{\}}
applied to the pair yields \texttt{6.\allowbreak{}0}. The raw value
bypasses both the \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} branches
because it has neither method.

This facility is convenient for scaling and similar operations, but it
has a sharp edge. The raw value is stored by value inside the
\texttt{BinExpr}, so it is copied in at construction time. For a
\texttt{double} this is free, but for a large raw value --- say, a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}float\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
passed on the right of an operator --- the copy can be expensive. More
importantly, the operation tag must be defined for the pair of types
involved:
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\{\}}
applied to a \texttt{double} and a \texttt{double} works, but applied to
a \texttt{Vec} and a \texttt{double} it requires a corresponding
\texttt{operator*(\allowbreak{}const Vec\&,\allowbreak{} double)\allowbreak{}},
which the user must supply.

The mixing of raw values and leaves also affects the return type of
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. When a raw \texttt{double} is
combined with a \texttt{Scalar} whose
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns
\texttt{double}, the result is \texttt{double}. When a raw \texttt{int}
is combined with a \texttt{Scalar}, the result follows the usual
arithmetic conversions:
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\{\}}
applied to \texttt{double} and \texttt{int} yields \texttt{double}. The
return type is always deduced from the actual
\texttt{Op\{\}(\allowbreak{}l,\allowbreak{} r)\allowbreak{}} invocation,
so the standard conversion rules apply directly.

\section{Pitfalls: Operand Lifetimes and Value
Semantics}\label{ch16-pitfalls-operand-lifetimes-and-value-semantics}

The node types store their operands by value. This is a deliberate
choice: it makes nodes self-contained, safe to return from functions,
and independent of the lifetime of the original leaves. A
\texttt{BinExpr} returned from a function carries its own copies of its
leaves, and can be evaluated long after the original leaves have gone
out of scope.

The cost of this safety is that leaves are copied into every node that
references them. A leaf that appears \texttt{n} times in an expression
is copied \texttt{n} times, once into each node. For a small leaf type
like \texttt{Scalar} this is negligible, but for a leaf wrapping a large
container the cost can be significant. Users for whom this cost matters
have two options: make the leaf cheap to copy (for example, by wrapping
a \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}shared\_\allowbreak{}ptr} to
the underlying data), or restructure the expression to reuse a single
sub-expression via \texttt{auto}.

\begin{code}
Scalar a{2}, b{3};
auto bc = b * c;          // a named sub-expression, evaluated once
auto expr = a + bc - bc;  // bc copied twice into the tree
\end{code}

The example above illustrates the trade-off. Naming \texttt{b * c} as
\texttt{bc} and then using \texttt{bc} twice in the outer expression
causes the \texttt{bc} node to be copied twice into the outer tree. Each
copy is a shallow copy of the \texttt{BinExpr}, which in turn copies its
\texttt{Scalar} operands. If \texttt{bc} is large, this is wasteful; if
it is small, it is harmless. There is no reference-mode alternative in
the library, so the trade-off must be managed by the user.

A related pitfall applies to leaf types that themselves hold references
or views. Although the node copies the leaf, the leaf's copy constructor
may produce a shallow copy that shares data with the original. If the
shared data is destroyed before the node is evaluated, the node will
hold a dangling reference. The library cannot protect against this; it
is the user's responsibility to ensure that leaf types are either
deep-copying or that the underlying data outlives the node.

The by-value storage model also means that mutating a leaf after
constructing an expression has no effect on the expression's result. The
expression captures the leaf's value at construction time, not a
reference to the leaf. This is usually the desired behaviour --- it
makes expressions referentially transparent --- but it can surprise
users who expect later mutations to be visible.

\section{Pitfalls: Deeply Nested
Types}\label{ch16-pitfalls-deeply-nested-types}

The type of a \texttt{BinExpr} expression grows with the depth of the
tree. A chain of \texttt{n} binary operators produces a type whose fully
expanded spelling has \texttt{O(\allowbreak{}n)\allowbreak{}} template
arguments nested to depth \texttt{O(\allowbreak{}n)\allowbreak{}}.
Compiler diagnostics that attempt to print this type can become
unreadable for even moderately sized expressions, and error messages
that mention the type by name can stretch for many lines.

The standard remedy is to use \texttt{auto} for every expression
variable. The \texttt{ExprTemplates} design is built around type
deduction: the operators return deduced types, the free-function
overloads return deduced types, and
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns a deduced type. There
is rarely a reason to name a \texttt{BinExpr} type explicitly, and doing
so should be avoided.

\begin{code}
Scalar a{1}, b{2}, c{3}, d{4};
auto expr = a + b - c + d;            // do not spell this type
auto r = expr.eval();
\end{code}

When a function must accept an arbitrary expression as a parameter, the
usual idiom is a template with a deduced type parameter, optionally
constrained by a concept. Because \texttt{ExprLike} constrains leaves
rather than nodes, a function accepting an arbitrary node must use a
weaker constraint, such as
\texttt{requires (\allowbreak{}const T\& t)\allowbreak{} \{ t.\allowbreak{}eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}; \}},
or simply be an unconstrained template. The library does not provide a
concept for ``any expression node'', so user code that needs one must
define it locally.

Deeply nested types can also affect compile times, particularly when the
same expression is instantiated many times in different contexts. The
cost is usually modest, but for expressions built inside tight generic
loops over many types it can become noticeable. As with all
template-heavy code, the remedy is to factor common expressions into
named functions whose bodies are compiled once.

\section{Pitfalls: The Operation Must Be
Defined}\label{ch16-pitfalls-the-operation-must-be-defined}

The \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin hardcodes the operation tags as
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}divides\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
These transparent functors dispatch to the corresponding operator
overloads found by normal lookup. If the value type returned by a leaf's
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} does not
support the required operator, the expression will fail to compile at
the point where \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is
instantiated.

\begin{code}
struct StringLeaf : dsl::DSL<StringLeaf, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  std::string s;
  std::string expr_value() const { return s; }
};

StringLeaf p{"hello"}, q{"world"};
auto expr = p + q;        // ok: std::string supports operator+
// auto bad = p * q;      // error: std::string has no operator*
\end{code}

The error message for an undefined operation is usually clear, because
it originates from the body of \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
where \texttt{Op\{\}(\allowbreak{}l,\allowbreak{} r)\allowbreak{}} is
instantiated. The fault is reported at the point where the operation is
attempted, not at the point where the tree is built, because the
operators that build the tree do not inspect the value type. This is
consistent with the lazy philosophy: construction never fails, only
evaluation does.

A consequence is that the set of usable operations on a given leaf type
is determined entirely by the value type's operator overloads. To extend
the set, the user provides additional operator overloads for the value
type; the \texttt{ExprTemplates} machinery picks them up automatically
because the standard tags are transparent. There is no need to register
operations with the library or to specialise the tags.

A subtler pitfall concerns the choice of value type returned by
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. If
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns by
value, as in the \texttt{Scalar} example, each call materialises a copy.
If it returns by reference, the leaf avoids the copy but exposes the
leaf's internal storage to the operation tag. For types where the
operation modifies its arguments --- which the standard tags do not ---
returning by reference would be a hazard. The standard tags are pure, so
the choice is purely one of performance.

\section{Summary}\label{ch16-summary}

\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}Op,\allowbreak{} L,\allowbreak{} R\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}Op,\allowbreak{} E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
are the two node types that back every expression built through the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ExprTemplates} feature introduced
in Chapter 15. Each stores its operands by value and carries its
operation as a type parameter, drawing on the standard tags
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}divides\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Evaluation is performed by a \texttt{constexpr}
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method that recursively walks
the tree in a single post-order pass, applying each operation tag on the
way back up.

The \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} helper, with its three-way
\texttt{if constexpr} dispatch on
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, and
fallback, unifies inner nodes, leaves, and raw values into a single
evaluation path. The result is a fused pass: no intermediate temporaries
are materialised for sub-expressions, and the entire tree is collapsed
into one value of the leaf's value type. The \texttt{ExprLike} concept,
built on the feature-tag machinery of Chapter 5, characterises the leaf
types that participate in this scheme.

The free-function operator overloads permit \texttt{BinExpr} nodes to
appear on the left of further operators, enabling left-associative
chains to be written naturally. The whole system is \texttt{constexpr},
so expression trees can be built and evaluated at compile time. The
principal pitfalls are the by-value storage of operands (which copies
leaves into every referencing node), the rapid growth of type spelling
with tree depth (which argues for \texttt{auto}), and the requirement
that the operation tag be defined for the leaf's value type (which is
the user's responsibility). With these caveats observed,
\texttt{BinExpr} and \texttt{UnaryExpr} provide a small, complete, and
composable foundation for lazy arithmetic over any type that satisfies
\texttt{ExprLike}.
